(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["advrag",{"l:advrag/late-interaction-retrieval-colbert-and-multi-vec":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A normal embedding model squeezes a whole passage into \u003cb>one vector\u003c/b>. \u003cb>Late interaction\u003c/b> keeps \u003cb>one small vector per token\u003c/b> and scores a document by letting every query token find its best-matching document token, then summing those best matches (\u003cb>MaxSim\u003c/b>). You get much of a cross-encoder's precision while still precomputing document vectors offline. The price is storage: tens to hundreds of vectors per passage instead of one.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Three ways to score a query against a passage\u003c/h4>\u003cp>You met two of these in earlier modules. A \u003cb>bi-encoder\u003c/b> embeds query and passage separately into single vectors and compares them with cosine similarity (the Embeddings module). A \u003cb>cross-encoder\u003c/b> reads query and passage together through one transformer and outputs a relevance score (the reranking lesson in the RAG module). Late interaction, introduced by \u003cb>ColBERT\u003c/b> (Khattab and Zaharia, 2020), sits between them.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Bi-encoder\u003c/th>\u003cth>Late interaction\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cross-encoder\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>What is stored per passage\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 vector\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 vector per token (often 128 dims)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nothing; computed at query time\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Query-time work\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1 dot product per candidate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Small matrix product per candidate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Full transformer pass per candidate\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Can search millions of docs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, with ANN\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, with special engines or as a rescorer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No, rerank a shortlist only\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fine-grained matching\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Weak; one vector blurs details\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Strong; each query term matched separately\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Strongest\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Storage\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Smallest\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Often 10 or more times larger\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The ColBERT paper reported quality competitive with BERT cross-encoder rankers while running about two orders of magnitude faster and using four orders of magnitude fewer FLOPs per query, because document token vectors are computed once at indexing time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>MaxSim, worked by hand\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Encode the query and the document into token vectors, normalise them, compute every query-token by document-token similarity, take the maximum in each row and add the rows up.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import numpy as np\n\ndef maxsim(Q: np.ndarray, D: np.ndarray) -&gt; float:\n    \"\"\"Q: (query_tokens, dim), D: (doc_tokens, dim), rows L2-normalised.\"\"\"\n    sims = Q @ D.T                         # every query token against every doc token\n    return float(sims.max(axis=1).sum())   # best match per query token, summed\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Query: \"reset password\". Two candidate documents, with made-up but realistic cosine similarities:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Doc A: \"How to change your login password\"\n               change  login  password    best\n  reset          0.71   0.32     0.40     0.71\n  password       0.25   0.58     0.97     0.97\n                                 score =  1.68\n\nDoc B: \"Reset the router to factory settings\"\n               reset  router  factory  settings   best\n  reset         0.98    0.20     0.15      0.30   0.98\n  password      0.35    0.22     0.10      0.28   0.35\n                                         score =  1.33\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Doc B contains the literal word \"reset\", but nothing in it matches \"password\", so it loses. Every query term has to find its own evidence. A single-vector model can be pulled towards Doc B by the strong shared topic of \"resetting\"; MaxSim cannot ignore the missing term.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The storage bill\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Late interaction trades memory for precision. A worked estimate for one million passages of about 100 tokens:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Single vector:   1,000,000 × 1,024 dims × 2 bytes (float16)      ≈ 2.0 GB\nColBERT tokens:  1,000,000 × 100 tokens × 128 dims × 2 bytes      ≈ 25.6 GB  (about 13×)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The field attacks this in three ways. \u003cb>ColBERTv2\u003c/b> (2021) added residual compression, storing each token vector as a centroid id plus a few bits of residual, and reported a 6 to 10 times smaller footprint. \u003cb>Token pooling\u003c/b> merges near-duplicate token vectors. And many systems use late interaction only as a \u003cb>rescorer\u003c/b>: a cheap first stage (BM25 plus single vectors) fetches 100 to 200 candidates, then MaxSim reorders them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Where it lives.\u003c/b> Qdrant stores multivectors natively with a \u003ccode>max_sim\u003c/code> comparator, and its query API can prefetch candidates with a dense vector and rescore them with the multivector. Vespa has built-in ColBERT support. The original \u003ccode>stanford-futuredata/ColBERT\u003c/code> repository includes the PLAID engine for full-corpus search, and PyLate trains and runs ColBERT-style models on top of sentence-transformers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>When it pays off.\u003c/b> Queries with several specific constraints (\"EU invoices over 10,000 euros that were disputed\"), domains with rare terms, and out-of-domain data where single-vector models generalise poorly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>When it does not.\u003c/b> Short FAQ-style corpora where hybrid search plus a cross-encoder reranker already hits your recall target. Measure before you pay the storage.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Pitfall.\u003c/b> Query length matters: MaxSim sums over query tokens, so long queries get larger raw scores. Compare scores only within one query, never across queries.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In one sentence each, how do a bi-encoder, a cross-encoder and a late-interaction model score a passage?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A bi-encoder compares one precomputed passage vector with one query vector; a cross-encoder runs query and passage through a transformer together and outputs a score; a late-interaction model compares precomputed per-token passage vectors with per-token query vectors and sums each query token's best match (MaxSim).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A query has 3 tokens. Their best similarities against a document are 0.9, 0.8 and 0.2. What is the MaxSim score and what does the 0.2 tell you?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>0.9 + 0.8 + 0.2 = 1.9. The 0.2 says one query term found no good match anywhere in the document, which is exactly the signal a single pooled vector tends to blur.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do many production systems use late interaction as a rescorer rather than the first stage?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Storing and searching one vector per token over the whole corpus is expensive in memory and needs specialised engines, while rescoring 100 to 200 candidates from a cheap first stage captures most of the precision gain at a fraction of the cost.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:advrag/visual-document-retrieval-colpali-and-pages-as-i":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Parsing a PDF into text loses the tables, charts and layout that often carry the answer. \u003cb>ColPali\u003c/b> skips parsing: it renders each page as an image, encodes it with a vision-language model into about a thousand \u003cb>patch vectors\u003c/b>, and matches query tokens against patches with ColBERT-style late interaction. On visually rich documents this beats text pipelines and is simpler to build. The costs are storage, page-level granularity and a VLM at answer time.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why text pipelines struggle\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A typical PDF pipeline runs layout detection, OCR, table extraction and chunking, then embeds the text (the RAG module covers this). Each step can fail: a bar chart becomes nothing, a table becomes a jumble of numbers, a two-column page is read in the wrong order. Some teams caption every figure with a VLM, which is slow and costly at indexing time. The ColPali authors built \u003cb>ViDoRe\u003c/b>, a benchmark of page-level retrieval over reports, slides, infographics and forms, and found these pipelines were the weak link.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How ColPali works\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>PDF page\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Render\u003c/b>\u003csmall>page image\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>VLM encoder\u003c/b>\u003csmall>1,024 patches + 6 text tokens\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Project to 128 dims\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Store 1,030 vectors per page\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>User query\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Language model\u003c/b>\u003csmall>one vector per query token\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>MaxSim against every page\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Top pages\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>VLM answers from page images\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The original model is built on \u003cb>PaliGemma-3B\u003c/b>: a SigLIP vision encoder splits the page into a 32 by 32 grid of \u003cb>1,024 patches\u003c/b>, the Gemma language model contextualises the patch embeddings, and a final layer projects each one to \u003cb>128 dimensions\u003c/b>. Six extra text-token vectors (\"Describe the image\" prompt tokens) bring it to 1,030 vectors per page. A query is embedded token by token, and the score is MaxSim, exactly as in the previous lesson. Because patches are matched individually, you can even highlight which parts of the page matched each query word: the paper shows the token \"hour\" lighting up a chart's time axis.\u003c/p>\u003cp>On ViDoRe, ColPali outperformed all evaluated text pipelines, including ones that captioned every figure with a strong proprietary VLM, with the biggest gains on infographics, figures and tables. Successors swap in stronger backbones; the paper itself trained a Qwen2-VL variant (ColQwen2) and found better VLMs make better retrievers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The cost, worked out\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The paper reports \u003cb>257.5 KB per page\u003c/b> (1,030 vectors × 128 dims × 2 bytes). For a corpus of 100,000 pages:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>ColPali, float16:          100,000 × 257.5 KB          ≈ 25.8 GB\nToken pooling, factor 3:   one third of the vectors    ≈ 8.6 GB  (paper: 97.8% of quality kept)\nBinary quantisation:       1,030 × 128 bits ≈ 16 KB    ≈ 1.6 GB  (then rescore top hits)\nText RAG for comparison:   ~2.1 chunks/page × 1,024 dims × 4 bytes  ≈ 0.9 GB\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Indexing is faster than you might expect, because rendering a page and running one forward pass replaces OCR, layout analysis and captioning. Query encoding took about 30 ms in the paper, and computing MaxSim added roughly 1 ms per 1,000 pages for small corpora; at millions of pages you need an engine built for multivectors (Vespa, Qdrant) or pooling and quantisation.\u003c/p>\u003cp>There is a second family worth knowing: \u003cb>single-vector multimodal embedders\u003c/b> (for example Voyage's and Cohere's multimodal embedding models, as of late 2026) that embed a page screenshot into one vector. They are far cheaper to store and plug into any vector database, usually at some cost in fine-grained matching.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tooling.\u003c/b> The authors' \u003ccode>colpali-engine\u003c/code> library (GitHub \u003ccode>illuin-tech/colpali\u003c/code>) trains and runs ColPali and ColQwen models; Byaldi wraps them in a few lines for prototypes; Vespa and Qdrant serve them at scale.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Answering.\u003c/b> Retrieval returns pages, so the generator must be a VLM that reads page images. Three page images can cost several thousand input tokens (see image token costs in the Multimodal module), so keep top-k small.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keep a text layer too.\u003c/b> Store any extractable text alongside the page vectors. You still want BM25 for part numbers and exact phrases, and you want quotable text for citations.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Granularity.\u003c/b> A hit is a whole page. If users need sentence-level citations, add a second step that locates the passage inside the retrieved page.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>When not to use it.\u003c/b> Clean, text-heavy documents (policies, wikis, code docs) gain little; standard text RAG is cheaper and easier to cite.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can ColPali beat a text pipeline on an annual report full of charts?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The text pipeline must convert charts and tables to text through OCR, layout analysis or captioning, and loses or garbles that information; ColPali embeds the page image directly, so visual content such as chart axes, legends and table structure is matched against the query patch by patch.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Estimate float16 storage for 40,000 pages with ColPali, and with factor-3 token pooling.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>40,000 × 257.5 KB ≈ 10.3 GB without pooling; pooling by a factor of 3 keeps a third of the vectors, so about 3.4 GB, at roughly 98% of the original retrieval quality according to the paper.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name two reasons to keep extracted text even when you retrieve with page images.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Exact-match keyword search (IDs, part numbers, names) still works best with BM25 over text, and quotable text spans make precise citations possible, while page-image retrieval only tells you which page matched.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:advrag/knowledge-graphs-for-llm-applications":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>knowledge graph\u003c/b> stores facts as \u003cb>entities\u003c/b> (nodes) joined by \u003cb>relations\u003c/b> (edges), each fact a \u003cb>triple\u003c/b>: subject, predicate, object. Graphs answer questions about \u003ci>connections\u003c/i>: multi-hop chains, dependencies, ownership, counts. LLMs make graphs cheaper to build by extracting triples from text, but extraction errors, entity duplicates and maintenance are the real costs. Use a graph when your questions are about relationships, not when they are lookups.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Triples and property graphs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Take two sentences from an engineering wiki: \"The checkout service calls the payments API, which stores its data in auth-db. Team Orion owns the payments API.\" As triples:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>(checkout-service) -[DEPENDS_ON]-&gt; (payments-api)\n(payments-api)     -[DEPENDS_ON]-&gt; (auth-db)\n(payments-api)     -[OWNED_BY]-&gt;   (team-orion)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two data models dominate. \u003cb>Property graphs\u003c/b> (Neo4j, Memgraph, FalkorDB, Amazon Neptune) give nodes a label (\u003ccode>Service\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>Team\u003c/code>) and key-value properties, and edges a type and properties; you query them with \u003cb>Cypher\u003c/b> or its ISO successor GQL. \u003cb>RDF triple stores\u003c/b> represent everything as triples with global identifiers and are queried with SPARQL; they are common in publishing, life sciences and government data. Most LLM application work uses property graphs.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Now the question \"Which services break if auth-db goes down, and who owns them?\" is one query:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>MATCH (s:Service)-[:DEPENDS_ON*1..3]-&gt;(:Database {name: \"auth-db\"})\nOPTIONAL MATCH (s)-[:OWNED_BY]-&gt;(t:Team)\nRETURN s.name, t.name\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Chunk retrieval would need to fetch both sentences, possibly from different pages, and hope the model chains them. With 3 hops and 50 services, it will not.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Building a graph with an LLM\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Chunks\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>LLM extraction\u003c/b>\u003csmall>typed triples as JSON\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Entity resolution\u003c/b>\u003csmall>merge duplicates\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Load\u003c/b>\u003csmall>MERGE into graph\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Query\u003c/b>\u003csmall>traversal, text-to-Cypher\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fix a schema first.\u003c/b> Allowed entity types and relation types, kept small. Open-ended extraction (\"extract all relations\") produces thousands of near-synonymous edge types that nobody can query.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Extract with structured output.\u003c/b> One call per chunk, returning validated objects:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from typing import Literal\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\n\nEntityType = Literal[\"Service\", \"Database\", \"Team\", \"Person\"]\n\nclass Triple(BaseModel):\n    subject: str\n    subject_type: EntityType\n    relation: Literal[\"DEPENDS_ON\", \"OWNED_BY\", \"ON_CALL_FOR\"]\n    object: str\n    object_type: EntityType\n\nclass Extraction(BaseModel):\n    triples: list[Triple]\n# Call the model with the chunk and the schema (structured outputs, see the Tools module),\n# then store every triple with the id of the chunk it came from, for provenance and citations.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>\u003cb>Resolve entities.\u003c/b> \"Payments API\", \"payments-api\" and \"the payment service\" must become one node. Normalise strings, compare name embeddings, let an LLM adjudicate close pairs, and link to existing IDs (a service catalogue, CRM account ids) whenever they exist. Under-merging fragments the graph; over-merging invents false facts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Load idempotently\u003c/b> with \u003ccode>MERGE\u003c/code>, so re-running extraction does not duplicate nodes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Tools that package this: Neo4j's LLM Graph Builder and \u003ccode>neo4j-graphrag\u003c/code> Python package, LlamaIndex's property graph index and LangChain's graph transformers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Querying: three patterns\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Pattern\u003c/th>\u003cth>How it works\u003c/th>\u003cth>Risk\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Text-to-Cypher\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The LLM writes a graph query from the schema and the question\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Wrong or expensive queries; run read-only, with timeouts and few-shot examples\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Entity-anchored traversal\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Find entities named in the question, pull their 1 to 2 hop neighbourhood plus linked chunks into context\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hubs with thousands of edges flood the context; cap neighbours\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Graph plus vectors\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Vector search finds chunks; the graph adds related entities and facts around them\u003c/td>\u003ctd>More moving parts to evaluate\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>When a graph helps (and when it does not)\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Question shape\u003c/th>\u003cth>Chunk RAG\u003c/th>\u003cth>Graph\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"What is our refund window?\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Good\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Overkill\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Which services depend, directly or indirectly, on auth-db?\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Poor\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Good\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"How many open incidents involve team Orion's services?\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Poor; cannot count reliably\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Good, with a count query\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Who approved the vendor that supplies plant 7?\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Poor unless one chunk says it\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Good\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Summarise the main themes of all incident reports\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Poor\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Good with community summaries (next lesson)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In practice the best graph is often one you \u003cb>already have\u003c/b>: a service catalogue, CMDB, CRM, org chart or product database. Querying it through a tool is cheaper and more accurate than extracting a graph from prose about it. Extract a graph from text only when the relationships exist nowhere else.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Write the triples in \"Maria leads the data team, which owns the billing warehouse.\"\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>(maria) -[LEADS]-&gt; (data-team) and (data-team) -[OWNS]-&gt; (billing-warehouse), with entity types such as Person, Team and Database.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is entity resolution and what happens if you skip it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Merging different mentions of the same real thing (\"Payments API\", \"payment service\") into one node. Without it the graph fragments into duplicates, so multi-hop queries miss paths and counts are wrong; done too aggressively, it merges different things and creates false facts.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give one question type where a graph clearly beats chunk retrieval and one where it does not.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A graph wins on multi-hop or aggregate questions such as \"which services depend on auth-db, directly or indirectly\"; it adds nothing for single-fact lookups such as \"what is the refund window\", where chunk retrieval is cheaper and simpler.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should a text-to-Cypher tool run with read-only credentials and a timeout?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The model can write wrong, destructive or very expensive queries (for example unbounded variable-length paths), so the database must make writes impossible and kill runaway queries.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:graphrag-for-global-questions":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Chunk retrieval cannot answer \"What are the main themes across these 5,000 documents?\" because no chunk contains that answer. \u003cb>GraphRAG\u003c/b> (Microsoft Research, 2024) uses an LLM to extract an entity graph, clusters it into nested \u003cb>communities\u003c/b>, writes a summary report for every community in advance, and answers global questions by \u003cb>map-reduce\u003c/b> over those reports. It wins clearly on whole-corpus sensemaking questions and costs far more to index than plain RAG.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why chunk retrieval fails on global questions\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Top-k retrieval returns the 10 chunks most similar to the question. \"What are the top themes in this data?\" is similar to nothing in particular, so you get 10 arbitrary chunks out of thousands and an answer about whatever they mention. The GraphRAG paper frames these as \u003cb>query-focused summarisation\u003c/b> over a whole corpus, not retrieval. The brute-force alternative, summarising every chunk at query time, is accurate but costs the whole corpus in tokens on every question.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The indexing pipeline\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Corpus\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Text units\u003c/b>\u003csmall>chunks\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>LLM\u003c/b>\u003csmall>extract entities, relations, claims\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Graph\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Leiden\u003c/b>\u003csmall>nested communities\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>LLM\u003c/b>\u003csmall>community reports\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Index\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Text units.\u003c/b> The corpus is chunked; chunks are the unit of provenance for citations.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Extraction.\u003c/b> For each chunk an LLM extracts entities (with descriptions), relationships between them and, optionally, claims. Descriptions of the same entity from different chunks are merged by another LLM call.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Communities.\u003c/b> The \u003cb>Leiden\u003c/b> algorithm partitions the graph into communities of densely connected entities, recursively, giving a hierarchy: a few broad communities at the root, many small ones at the leaves.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Community reports.\u003c/b> An LLM writes a report for every community at every level: its key entities, relationships and findings. This is the expensive, distinctive step.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Query modes\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Mode\u003c/th>\u003cth>Uses\u003c/th>\u003cth>Best for\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Global\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Community reports at a chosen level, map-reduce\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"What are the main themes?\", \"What are the biggest risks across all reports?\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Local\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Entities in the question plus their neighbours, relations and source chunks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Who is Scrooge and what are his main relationships?\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>DRIFT\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Local search enriched with community context\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Specific questions that need broader context\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Basic\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Plain top-k vector search\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Simple lookups; your baseline\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Global search\u003c/b> works like this: community reports are shuffled into batches; in the \u003cb>map\u003c/b> step each batch produces an intermediate answer as a list of points, each with an importance score; in the \u003cb>reduce\u003c/b> step the highest-rated points are combined into the final answer. Lower (more detailed) community levels give more thorough answers but more map calls. A global query can therefore cost dozens of LLM calls.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What the paper found\u003c/h4>\u003cp>On two datasets of about one million tokens each (podcast transcripts and news articles) and 125 generated sensemaking questions per comparison, GraphRAG's global approaches beat vector RAG on \u003cb>comprehensiveness\u003c/b> in 72 to 83% of head-to-head judgements and on \u003cb>diversity\u003c/b> in 62 to 82%, while vector RAG won on \u003cb>directness\u003c/b> (short, to-the-point answers), the control criterion. Root-level community summaries needed over 97% fewer context tokens than summarising the source text directly, at a modest loss in quality. Indexing the podcast set took 281 minutes with GPT-4-turbo and produced a graph of 8,564 nodes and 20,691 edges.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What it costs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The docs open with a warning that GraphRAG \"can consume a lot of LLM resources\", and estimate graph extraction at about 75% of indexing cost. A rough, illustrative estimate:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Corpus 1,000,000 tokens in 1,200-token chunks          ≈ 900 chunks\nExtraction: 900 calls × (4,000 in + 1,000 out)         ≈ 3.6M in, 0.9M out\nSummaries and reports (extraction ≈ 75% of the total)  ≈ total 4.8M in, 1.2M out\nSmall model at $1 in / $5 out per million              ≈ $4.80 + $6.00 ≈ $11\nLarge model at $5 in / $25 out per million             ≈ $24 + $30   ≈ $54\nEmbedding the same corpus for plain RAG                ≈ 1M tokens: cents\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The dollars look small for one corpus, but they recur on every re-index, scale linearly with corpus size, and the hours of wall-clock time and rate-limit pressure are often the real blocker. Two cheaper options from Microsoft: \u003cb>FastGraphRAG\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>graphrag index --method fast\u003c/code>) extracts noun phrases with classic NLP libraries and links entities that co-occur in a chunk, using the LLM only for community reports, at the price of a noisier graph; and \u003cb>LazyGraphRAG\u003c/b> (a research variant) skips up-front summarisation entirely and does the LLM work at query time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Use it for sensemaking\u003c/b>: investigations, research synthesis, \"what is going on across all of these reports\". Do not use it as a faster lookup engine; basic RAG answers lookups better and cheaper.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tune the prompts.\u003c/b> The extraction prompts are generic; the docs strongly recommend running prompt tuning (\u003ccode>graphrag prompt-tune\u003c/code>) to adapt entity types and examples to your domain.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Plan updates.\u003c/b> The \u003ccode>graphrag update\u003c/code> command adds documents incrementally, but community structure can shift, so schedule periodic full re-indexes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Permissions.\u003c/b> A community report summarises many documents. If those documents have different access rights, the report leaks across them (see the production concerns lesson).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A simpler baseline to beat.\u003c/b> For a few hundred documents, a plain map-reduce summary (summarise each document once, then summarise the summaries per question) can be competitive. Measure it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When does a knowledge graph help over chunk retrieval?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When answering requires connecting information spread across many chunks: multi-hop questions (A depends on B which depends on C), aggregation and counting over entities, questions about relationships between people, systems or organisations, and global questions about a whole corpus such as main themes, which GraphRAG answers from precomputed community summaries. For single-fact lookups that one passage answers, chunk retrieval is cheaper, simpler and usually more direct.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Describe GraphRAG's global search in three steps.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Load the community reports from a chosen level of the hierarchy; map: send batches of reports with the question to an LLM, which returns rated key points; reduce: keep the highest-rated points and ask the LLM to combine them into the final answer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why did vector RAG still win on directness in the GraphRAG paper, and why does that matter?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Its answers come from a few closely matching chunks, so they are short and to the point, whereas global answers synthesise many communities. For lookup-style questions users want directness, which is why you keep basic search as a mode and route by question type.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your corpus doubles in size every quarter. What is the main operational risk of GraphRAG?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Indexing cost and time grow with the corpus and recur on every re-index, community reports go stale as new data arrives, and incremental updates can shift the community structure, so you need a budget and schedule for re-indexing or a cheaper variant such as FastGraphRAG.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:advrag/lighter-graph-approaches-lightrag-entity-linking":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Full GraphRAG is batch-built and expensive. Lighter designs use the graph as an \u003cb>index\u003c/b> rather than a pile of summaries: \u003cb>LightRAG\u003c/b> extracts entities and relations once and retrieves at two levels (specific entities and broad relations), and supports incremental updates. \u003cb>Entity linking\u003c/b> keeps any graph usable by mapping every mention to one canonical node. \u003cb>Temporal knowledge graphs\u003c/b> such as Graphiti record \u003ci>when\u003c/i> each fact was true, which is what agent memory and changing business data need.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>LightRAG: dual-level retrieval\u003c/h4>\u003cp>LightRAG (Guo et al., 2024) indexes like GraphRAG's first half: an LLM extracts entities and relationships from each chunk, and each entity and relation gets a short description that is embedded. It skips community detection and community reports. At query time:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>An LLM extracts two kinds of keywords from the question: \u003cb>low-level\u003c/b> (specific names and things, \"Scrooge\", \"auth-db\") and \u003cb>high-level\u003c/b> (themes, \"redemption\", \"outage causes\").\u003c/li>\u003cli>Low-level keywords are matched against entity vectors; high-level keywords against relation vectors.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Matched entities and relations pull in their graph neighbours and the source chunks, which become the context.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>The README lists five query modes: \u003ccode>local\u003c/code> (entity-centred), \u003ccode>global\u003c/code> (relation and theme-centred), \u003ccode>hybrid\u003c/code> (both), \u003ccode>naive\u003c/code> (plain chunk vectors) and \u003ccode>mix\u003c/code> (graph plus chunk vectors, the default). Because there are no community reports to rebuild, new documents are merged into the existing graph incrementally. The README also gives practical advice worth copying: extraction runs on every chunk, so use a fast, cheap, non-reasoning model for it, and enabling a reranker improves quality at a cost of about one to two seconds.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>HippoRAG\u003c/b> (2024) takes another light route: it builds a graph of entities from the corpus and, at query time, runs Personalized PageRank from the entities in the question to find related passages in one step. The authors reported up to 20% gains on multi-hop QA, and single-step retrieval comparable to iterative methods while 10 to 30 times cheaper.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Entity linking\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Whatever graph you build, mentions must resolve to nodes. A worked example from contracts:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Mentions: \"Acme Ltd\", \"ACME\", \"Acme Limited (the Supplier)\", \"Acme Corp\" (in a US contract)\nRule 1  normalise: lowercase, drop legal suffixes and bracketed roles -&gt; all four become \"acme\"\nRule 2  same name and same jurisdiction (from contract metadata) -&gt; merge the three UK mentions\nRule 3  same name, different jurisdiction: ask an LLM (or a person) with both contexts\n        \"Is Acme Corp the same legal entity as Acme Ltd?\" -&gt; No, a US subsidiary\nResult  org:acme-uk (3 mentions), org:acme-us (1 mention), each linked to its CRM id\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The general recipe: cheap normalisation first, embedding similarity of name plus description to find candidates, an LLM or a human to adjudicate the ambiguous pairs, and links to authoritative IDs (company registry numbers, CRM accounts, employee IDs) whenever possible.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Temporal knowledge graphs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Facts change. \"Alice reports to Bob\" was true until June, then \"Alice reports to Carol\". A naive graph either keeps both edges (contradiction) or overwrites the old one (history lost). \u003cb>Graphiti\u003c/b>, the open-source engine behind Zep's agent memory, uses a \u003cb>bi-temporal\u003c/b> model: each edge records when the fact was valid in the world and when the system learned it. New information \u003cb>invalidates\u003c/b> old edges by closing their validity period instead of deleting them, so you can ask both \"Who is Alice's manager?\" and \"Who was her manager in March?\".\u003c/p>\u003cp>Graphiti ingests data as \u003cb>episodes\u003c/b> (a message, a document, a JSON record) incrementally, and retrieves with a hybrid of vector similarity, BM25 and graph traversal, with no LLM in the retrieval loop, which keeps lookups fast. The Zep paper reported 94.8% versus 93.4% for MemGPT on the Deep Memory Retrieval benchmark, and on LongMemEval accuracy gains of up to 18.5% with 90% lower latency than the baseline implementations. The Memory module covers how this fits into agent memory design.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Comparing the options\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>GraphRAG\u003c/th>\u003cth>LightRAG\u003c/th>\u003cth>Graphiti\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Built for\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Static corpus sensemaking\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Corpus QA with graph context\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Changing facts, agent memory\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Index cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Highest: extraction plus community reports\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium: extraction only\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium: extraction per episode\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Updates\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Batch; periodic re-index\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Incremental merge\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Incremental, with fact invalidation\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Query-time LLM calls\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Many for global search\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Keyword extraction plus answer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None for retrieval\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Strong at\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Main themes across everything\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Entity and relation questions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"What is true now, and what was true then?\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Start with the lightest option that answers your failing questions. Many teams run LightRAG or a simple entity index next to hybrid search and reserve GraphRAG for a few analyst workflows.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Budget extraction quality: graph errors are silent. Sample 50 extracted triples and check them by hand before trusting the graph.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep provenance on every node and edge (source chunk, timestamp) so answers can cite and stale facts can be traced.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does LightRAG skip compared with GraphRAG, and what does that buy?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It skips community detection and community reports. Indexing is cheaper and new documents can be merged incrementally, at the cost of weaker answers to whole-corpus \"main themes\" questions that GraphRAG's precomputed summaries handle best.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a bi-temporal graph invalidate edges instead of deleting them?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>So the graph keeps history: it can answer what is true now and what was true at an earlier time, and it can explain contradictions, which agent memory and business data with changing facts need.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name three steps of a practical entity-linking pipeline.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Normalise names (case, punctuation, legal suffixes); find candidate matches with embedding similarity of names and descriptions; adjudicate ambiguous pairs with an LLM or a human, and link to authoritative IDs where they exist.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:agentic-rag":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>In classic RAG your code retrieves once, using the user's words, before the model sees anything. In \u003cb>agentic RAG\u003c/b>, retrieval is a \u003cb>tool\u003c/b> the model calls: it decides whether to search, writes its own queries, reads the results, searches again if something is missing, and stops when it has enough. This handles vague and multi-hop questions much better, at the cost of more model calls, more latency and a new failure mode: not knowing when to stop.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>From pipeline to loop\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Question\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model\u003c/b>\u003csmall>answer or search?\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>search_docs(query)\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Results with ids\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model\u003c/b>\u003csmall>enough evidence?\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Answer with citations\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A question like \"Did the 2025 price change affect customers on the legacy EU plan?\" needs at least two searches (the price change, the legacy plan's terms) and a query that the user never typed. A single retrieval with the raw question usually finds only one half. The IRCoT paper showed the effect on multi-hop QA benchmarks: interleaving retrieval with reasoning steps improved retrieval by up to 21 points and answer accuracy by up to 15 points over one-step retrieve-and-read.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A minimal agentic RAG loop\u003c/h4>\u003cp>This uses the OpenAI-compatible tool-calling shape from the Tools module; \u003ccode>search\u003c/code> is your hybrid search from earlier modules.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\nTOOLS = [{\n    \"type\": \"function\",\n    \"function\": {\n        \"name\": \"search_docs\",\n        \"description\": \"Hybrid search over the product docs. Returns up to k passages with ids. \"\n                       \"Use short, specific queries; search again with different words if results miss.\",\n        \"parameters\": {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"properties\": {\n                \"query\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                \"k\": {\"type\": \"integer\", \"minimum\": 1, \"maximum\": 10},\n            },\n            \"required\": [\"query\"],\n        },\n    },\n}]\nSYSTEM = (\"Answer from the docs only. Search before answering factual questions. \"\n          \"Cite passage ids like [p12]. If the docs do not contain the answer, say so.\")\n\ndef answer(question: str, max_searches: int = 5) -&gt; str:\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": SYSTEM}, {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]\n    seen, searches = set(), 0\n    while searches &lt; max_searches:\n        msg = client.chat.completions.create(\n            model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", messages=messages, tools=TOOLS).choices[0].message\n        if not msg.tool_calls:\n            return msg.content                        # the model chose to stop\n        messages.append(msg)\n        for call in msg.tool_calls:\n            args = json.loads(call.function.arguments)\n            hits = search(args[\"query\"], k=args.get(\"k\", 5))\n            new = [h for h in hits if h[\"id\"] not in seen]\n            seen.update(h[\"id\"] for h in hits)\n            searches += 1\n            messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call.id,\n                             \"content\": json.dumps(new or \"No new passages for this query.\")})\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Search budget used. Answer now from what you \"\n                     \"found, and state clearly what you could not find.\"})\n    final = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", messages=messages,\n                                           tools=TOOLS, tool_choice=\"none\")   # answer, no more calls\n    return final.choices[0].message.content\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Three details matter more than the loop itself: the \u003cb>tool description\u003c/b> teaches the model how to search, results carry \u003cb>ids\u003c/b> so the answer can cite them, and the loop has a \u003cb>budget\u003c/b> with a forced final answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Designing the retrieval tools\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Several narrow tools beat one vague one.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>search_docs(query, product?, after_date?)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>read_document(doc_id, section?)\u003c/code> to fetch a whole section around a hit, and \u003ccode>run_sql(query)\u003c/code> for numbers. Filters as parameters let the model narrow a search instead of rewriting it endlessly.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Compact results.\u003c/b> Return id, title, date and a 100 to 300 word snippet, not whole documents. The model can call \u003ccode>read_document\u003c/code> when it needs more.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Helpful empty results.\u003c/b> \"No results for X. Try broader terms or check the product name\" steers the next query better than an empty list.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Query planning.\u003c/b> For comparison and multi-part questions, ask the model to list the sub-questions first, then search for each, in parallel when the API supports parallel tool calls.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stopping rules\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Without a stopping rule an agent either answers after one weak search or searches forever. Combine several:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Rule\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Step budget\u003c/td>\u003ctd>At most 5 searches, then force an answer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Token or cost budget\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stop when the conversation exceeds 30,000 tokens\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>No new evidence\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two consecutive searches return only already-seen passage ids\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sufficiency check\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model must state which sub-questions are answered before it stops\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Explicit give-up\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The prompt allows \"the docs do not say\" as a valid final answer\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost and latency.\u003c/b> Each round trip adds a model call (often 1 to 3 seconds). Use a fast model for the search loop, a stronger one only for the final answer if needed, and parallel tool calls for independent sub-queries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Route.\u003c/b> Simple lookups do not need a loop. A cheap classifier or the first model turn can decide between one-shot RAG and agentic search.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Agentic search without vectors.\u003c/b> Coding agents often skip embeddings entirely and search a repository with grep, glob and file reads, iterating like this loop. For corpora with exact identifiers (code, logs, configs) that can beat a vector index.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evaluate the loop\u003c/b>, not just the answer: number of searches, repeated queries, answers given without any search, and cost per question. The Agents and Loop engineering modules cover loop design in depth.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you let an agent decide when and what to retrieve?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Expose retrieval as a tool with a clear description and parameters (query, filters, k), and run a tool-calling loop: the model either answers or calls the search tool with a query it writes; your code runs the search and returns compact results with ids; the model reads them and decides to search again or answer with citations. Instructions tell it to search before answering factual questions and allow \"not found\", and the loop enforces a search budget, detects when searches stop returning new evidence and forces a final answer when the budget is spent.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does one-shot retrieval with the user's question fail on multi-hop questions?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>What you need to retrieve second depends on what you found first, and the user's words rarely contain the intermediate entity, so a single query retrieves at most one part of the evidence chain.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give three stopping rules for an agentic search loop.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A maximum number of searches or tokens, stopping when consecutive searches return no new passages, and a sufficiency check where the model confirms each sub-question is answered, with a forced final answer that states what is missing when a budget runs out.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name two signs in your traces that an agentic RAG loop is misbehaving.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The same or near-identical query repeated several times, and answers produced with no search at all on factual questions; others include very high search counts on simple questions and ignoring retrieved evidence.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:advrag/deep-research-systems":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>deep research\u003c/b> system is an agent that spends minutes and hundreds of thousands of tokens on one question: it \u003cb>plans\u003c/b>, \u003cb>searches\u003c/b> (often with parallel sub-agents), \u003cb>reads\u003c/b> full sources, \u003cb>synthesises\u003c/b> a long report and \u003cb>cites\u003c/b> every claim. It is agentic RAG scaled up, and the hard parts are managing context across many sources, knowing when to stop, keeping citations honest and keeping the bill under control.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The five stages\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Question\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Plan\u003c/b>\u003csmall>scope, sub-questions\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Search\u003c/b>\u003csmall>parallel workers\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Read\u003c/b>\u003csmall>fetch, extract, compress\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Synthesise\u003c/b>\u003csmall>outline, write\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Cite\u003c/b>\u003csmall>map claims to sources\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Report\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Plan.\u003c/b> Clarify scope (some products ask the user a few clarifying questions first), split the question into sub-questions and save the plan outside the context window so it survives truncation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Search.\u003c/b> Run web search, internal search or MCP tools for each sub-question. Start broad, then narrow.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Read.\u003c/b> Fetch full pages or documents, not just snippets; extract the passages that matter and compress them, usually with a cheaper model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Synthesise.\u003c/b> Write an outline from the compressed findings, then the report section by section. Decide whether gaps justify another research round.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cite.\u003c/b> Attach each claim to a specific source passage, then verify that the passage supports it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Orchestrator and workers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Anthropic's Research feature, described in its 2025 engineering post, is the best-documented production design. A \u003cb>lead agent\u003c/b> plans and saves the plan to memory (its context could be truncated past 200,000 tokens), then spins up \u003cb>3 to 5 sub-agents in parallel\u003c/b>, each with its own context window and a distinct sub-question; each sub-agent uses 3 or more tools in parallel. Sub-agents return condensed findings, the lead decides whether more research is needed, and a final \u003cb>citation agent\u003c/b> maps claims in the report to specific source locations.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Their reported numbers explain both the appeal and the cost:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Finding (Anthropic, 2025)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Number\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Multi-agent (Opus 4 lead, Sonnet 4 workers) versus single-agent Opus 4 on an internal research eval\u003c/td>\u003ctd>90.2% better\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Share of performance variance on BrowseComp explained by token usage alone\u003c/td>\u003ctd>80%\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Token use of a single agent versus a chat\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 4×\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Token use of the multi-agent system versus a chat\u003c/td>\u003ctd>about 15×\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Research time saved by parallelising sub-agents and tool calls\u003c/td>\u003ctd>up to 90% on complex queries\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The lesson: these systems work largely because they spend more tokens, in parallel, in separate context windows. That only pays when the question is valuable. The Multi-agent module covers orchestration patterns in general; here the focus is retrieval.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Context management is the core problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A research run touches far more text than fits in any context window. Working designs share these habits:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Workers compress.\u003c/b> A sub-agent reads 20 pages and returns one or two thousand tokens of findings with source ids, not raw pages.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Raw material lives outside the context.\u003c/b> Fetched pages are stored in files or a database; the context holds references and summaries.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Persisted plan and notes.\u003c/b> The plan and running findings are written to memory so a truncated or restarted agent can continue.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deduplication.\u003c/b> Track visited URLs and sources so workers do not research the same thing twice.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Build or buy (late 2026)\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Hosted options run research as long \u003cb>background jobs\u003c/b> that you start and then poll: Google's Gemini Deep Research agent (in preview in the Gemini API), and OpenAI's Responses API, where the dedicated deep-research model ids were retired in mid-2026 in favour of general models with web search, file search and MCP tools. Consumer assistants such as Claude and ChatGPT ship research modes built on the same pattern. Open-source implementations such as LangChain's \u003ccode>open_deep_research\u003c/code> (a LangGraph supervisor with researcher sub-agents and a compression step) and GPT Researcher let you plug in your own search tools and internal data. Build your own when the sources are internal, the report format is specific, or you need control over cost and data flow.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Evaluating and hardening\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Benchmarks.\u003c/b> BrowseComp (OpenAI, 2025) has 1,266 questions whose short answers are hard to find but easy to verify; Deep Research Bench has 100 PhD-level report tasks. For your own system, use 20 to 50 real research questions with rubric-based LLM judging (coverage, accuracy, source quality) plus human review of a sample.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Citation checks.\u003c/b> Automatically confirm that each cited passage exists and supports its claim; unsupported citations are the most damaging failure.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Source quality.\u003c/b> Prefer primary sources; penalise SEO content farms and AI-generated pages in ranking.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prompt injection.\u003c/b> Every fetched web page is untrusted input that can carry instructions. Workers should not have write tools, and the Security module's defences apply.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Budgets.\u003c/b> Cap sub-agents, searches per worker and total tokens per run; show the user progress and cost.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name the five stages of a deep research pipeline.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Plan (scope and sub-questions), search (per sub-question, often in parallel), read (fetch full sources and compress the relevant parts), synthesise (outline and write the report, deciding whether more research is needed) and cite (map each claim to a source passage and verify it).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do sub-agents return compressed findings instead of the pages they read?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The lead agent's context window is finite and quality degrades as it fills, so each worker uses its own context to read widely and hands back only the essential findings with source ids, letting the system cover far more material than one context could hold.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">According to Anthropic's analysis, what mostly explains research-agent performance, and what is the business implication?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Token usage, which alone explained 80% of the variance on BrowseComp. Multi-agent research is expensive (about 15 times the tokens of a chat), so it should be reserved for questions valuable enough to justify the cost, with budgets enforced.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:multimodal-rag":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Real corpora mix text with photos, diagrams, charts and scanned pages. There are three ways to make them searchable: \u003cb>convert everything to text\u003c/b> (captions, OCR, descriptions) and use normal RAG; \u003cb>embed images and text into one shared space\u003c/b> and search both with one query; or \u003cb>embed whole page images\u003c/b> (ColPali-style) and let a vision-language model read the retrieved pages. Most teams start with the first and add the others where the eval shows visual content is being missed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Three strategies compared\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Convert to text\u003c/th>\u003cth>Shared embedding space\u003c/th>\u003cth>Page images\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ingest\u003c/td>\u003ctd>VLM captions each image, OCR for scans\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Embed images with an image encoder, text with a text encoder in the same space\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Render pages, embed with ColPali or a multimodal embedder\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieval\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your existing hybrid search\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Vector search over both modalities\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Multivector or single-vector search over pages\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Generation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Any text model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>VLM if you pass the images\u003c/td>\u003ctd>VLM, always\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Strong for\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Mixed documents, citations, cheap queries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Photo and product catalogues, \"find the picture of...\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Slides, reports, forms, infographics\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Weak for\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Captions miss details the question later needs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Long text; the modality gap\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Storage, page-level citations\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Indexing cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One VLM call per image\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One encoder pass per item\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One VLM pass per page\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Strategy 1: convert to text\u003c/h4>\u003cp>At ingest, send each image to a VLM with a prompt such as \"Describe this figure for search: what it shows, every label, number and axis title\". Store the description as a text chunk with a pointer to the original image (\u003ccode>image_uri\u003c/code>, page number). At answer time, the retrieved description can be enough; for questions about details, pass the original image to a VLM too. This reuses all your text infrastructure, BM25 included, and keeps citations simple. The catch: a caption only contains what the captioning prompt thought to mention.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Strategy 2: a shared embedding space\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>CLIP\u003c/b> (OpenAI, 2021) trained an image encoder and a text encoder on 400 million image-caption pairs so that matching pairs end up close together. A text query can then retrieve images directly (the Embeddings module introduced this). Two practical problems:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Short text side.\u003c/b> CLIP's text encoder handles only 77 tokens, so it is poor at embedding long text chunks. Embed text chunks with your normal text model and use the image model only for images.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Modality gap.\u003c/b> In CLIP-style spaces, text-to-text similarities run systematically higher than text-to-image ones (Liang et al., 2022, \"Mind the gap\"). In a single mixed index, a text query returns text first and images rarely surface.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>The robust pattern is therefore \u003cb>one index per modality, fused by rank\u003c/b>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from pathlib import Path\nfrom PIL import Image\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\nclip = SentenceTransformer(\"clip-ViT-B-32\")                  # images and short text, one space\nimage_paths = sorted(Path(\"images\").glob(\"*.png\"))\nimage_ids = [f\"img:{p.stem}\" for p in image_paths]            # aligned with the rows of img_vecs\nimg_vecs = clip.encode([Image.open(p) for p in image_paths], normalize_embeddings=True)\n\ndef search_all(query: str, k: int = 5) -&gt; list[str]:\n    q = clip.encode([query], normalize_embeddings=True)[0]\n    top_images = [image_ids[i] for i in (img_vecs @ q).argsort()[::-1][:k]]\n    top_text = hybrid_text_search(query, k=k)                 # your BM25 + text-embedding search\n    return rrf(top_text, top_images)[:k]                      # reciprocal rank fusion\n\ndef rrf(*rankings: list[str], c: int = 60) -&gt; list[str]:\n    scores: dict[str, float] = {}\n    for ranking in rankings:\n        for rank, item in enumerate(ranking, start=1):\n            scores[item] = scores.get(item, 0.0) + 1.0 / (c + rank)\n    return sorted(scores, key=scores.get, reverse=True)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Rank fusion ignores raw scores, so the modality gap no longer matters; you can also reserve slots (for example at least one image in the top five) when the product needs it. Newer \u003cb>document-oriented multimodal embedders\u003c/b> (as of late 2026, for example Voyage's multimodal models and Cohere's Embed v4) embed screenshots of pages, slides and figures directly, and some accept interleaved text and images, such as a slide image with its title; they are designed for mixed text-and-image retrieval, but still check the gap on your own data.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Strategy 3: page images\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Covered in the ColPali lesson: best when meaning lives in layout, tables and charts. Pair it with a VLM generator and keep top-k small, because every retrieved page becomes one to a few thousand image tokens.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Generating the answer\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Put retrieved text and images in one multimodal message, each labelled with an id (\u003ccode>[img3]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[p12]\u003c/code>), and instruct the model to cite ids. Ask it to say when an image is illegible rather than guess. Image inputs dominate cost, so resize to what the question needs (the Multimodal module shows how image tokens are counted).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Start with captions\u003c/b> unless the eval says otherwise; they are cheap to add to an existing text pipeline.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Video and audio\u003c/b> usually reduce to the same patterns: transcribe speech, sample key frames, caption or embed them, keep timestamps for citations.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evaluate per modality.\u003c/b> Label which questions need an image to answer and measure recall@k on that slice separately, or text results will hide image failures.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you index images and text together and retrieve both for one query?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Pick a strategy per content type. Either caption or OCR every image with a VLM and index the descriptions as text chunks linked to the original image, so one text search covers both; or embed images with a CLIP-style or multimodal embedding model while text chunks keep their text embeddings and BM25, run the query against each index and merge the ranked lists with reciprocal rank fusion (possibly reserving slots for images); or embed page screenshots with a ColPali-style model. In every case store ids and source pointers, pass retrieved images plus text to a VLM for the answer, and measure recall separately on questions that need images.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the modality gap and how does rank fusion get around it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>In CLIP-style shared spaces, text-to-text similarity scores are systematically higher than text-to-image scores, so in one mixed index text crowds out images. Fusing separate per-modality rankings with RRF uses only rank positions, so each modality's best results compete fairly regardless of score scale.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why not embed long text chunks with CLIP's text encoder?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It accepts only 77 tokens and was trained on short captions, so it truncates and poorly represents long passages; a dedicated text embedding model does that job far better.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:advrag/production-concerns-freshness-permissions-and-de":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A retrieval index is a \u003cb>copy\u003c/b> of your source systems. Copies go stale, can show documents to people who could not open the original, and keep data you were obliged to delete. Treat ingestion as a \u003cb>sync pipeline\u003c/b> with change detection, store each document's permissions with its chunks and \u003cb>filter inside the retrieval query\u003c/b>, and make deletion cascade to every derived store: vectors, keyword index, caches, summaries and graph nodes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Freshness: incremental indexing\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Re-indexing everything nightly works for a few thousand documents and stops working after that. An incremental pipeline has three parts:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Change detection.\u003c/b> Webhooks or change feeds from the source (Confluence, Google Drive, SharePoint, databases via change data capture), or polling by modified time, plus a content hash so unchanged documents are skipped.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Content-addressed chunks.\u003c/b> Give each chunk an id derived from its document and its text hash. When a document changes, only new chunks are embedded, vanished chunks are deleted and unchanged ones stay.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Metadata-only updates.\u003c/b> Permission or title changes update payload fields without re-embedding.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import hashlib\n\ndef chunk_id(doc_id: str, text: str) -&gt; str:\n    return f\"{doc_id}:{hashlib.sha256(text.encode()).hexdigest()[:16]}\"\n\ndef sync_document(doc, index):          # sketch: `index` wraps your vector store and BM25 index\n    wanted = {chunk_id(doc.id, c): c for c in split(doc.text)}\n    existing = set(index.ids_for_doc(doc.id))\n    index.delete(list(existing - wanted.keys()))                  # edited or removed text\n    index.upsert([{\"id\": i, \"text\": wanted[i], \"vector\": embed(wanted[i]),\n                   \"doc_id\": doc.id, \"acl\": doc.allowed_groups,\n                   \"embed_model\": EMBED_MODEL, \"source_updated\": doc.modified}\n                  for i in wanted.keys() - existing])             # only new text is embedded\n    index.set_payload(doc_id=doc.id, acl=doc.allowed_groups)      # permission changes, no re-embed\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Store the embedding model name with every vector. Changing the model means re-embedding everything: build a new index alongside the old one, run your retrieval eval on both, then switch an alias (blue-green re-indexing). Track \u003cb>freshness lag\u003c/b> (source change to searchable) as a metric, and show \"last updated\" dates in citations.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Permissions: filter in the query\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The rule: \u003cb>a user must never retrieve a chunk they could not open in the source system.\u003c/b> Mirror source ACLs (users and groups allowed) onto every chunk, resolve the user's groups at query time, and apply the filter \u003cb>inside\u003c/b> the search:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">sql\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>SELECT id, text\nFROM chunks\nWHERE tenant_id = $1\n  AND allowed_groups &amp;&amp; $2::text[]      -- the user's groups overlap the chunk's ACL\nORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $3               -- pgvector cosine distance\nLIMIT 10;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Why not retrieve the top 10 and drop forbidden ones afterwards? Post-filtering can leave zero results when the top hits are all forbidden, and every forbidden hit that reached a reranker, log or cache is already a leak. Vector databases support filtered search natively (pgvector \u003ccode>WHERE\u003c/code> clauses, Qdrant and Weaviate payload filters); for hard tenant isolation use separate collections or partitions per tenant (the Embeddings module covers multi-tenancy).\u003c/p>\u003cp>The subtle leaks come from \u003cb>derived artefacts\u003c/b>:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Summaries, community reports and graph nodes\u003c/b> combine many documents. A GraphRAG report built from HR and engineering documents is readable only by people allowed to read all of them. Build derived artefacts per permission scope, or exclude restricted documents from them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Caches.\u003c/b> A semantic cache keyed only on the question will serve one user's answer, built from their documents, to another user. Key caches on the permission set too.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Conversation memory\u003c/b> can carry a restricted fact into a later answer for someone else in a shared thread.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Permission drift.\u003c/b> If ACL sync runs daily, a revoked user keeps access for up to a day. Sync permission changes faster than content changes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Deletion that actually deletes\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Under privacy law (for example the GDPR right to erasure) and customer contracts, deleting a document must remove it everywhere:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Store\u003c/th>\u003cth>What deletion must do\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Vector index\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Delete points; know that HNSW-based stores often mark deletions as tombstones and clean up on compaction\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Keyword index\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Delete the documents from BM25 or search engine indexes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Caches\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Invalidate semantic and response caches that used the document\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Derived artefacts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Regenerate summaries, community reports and graph facts that drew on it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Logs, traces and eval sets\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Apply retention rules; remove or redact copies\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Backups\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Follow a documented retention period\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Embeddings are not anonymous: research on \u003cb>embedding inversion\u003c/b> shows text can be partially reconstructed from vectors (OWASP lists this risk), so treat vectors of personal data as personal data.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Test deletion and permissions like features.\u003c/b> Plant a document containing a unique canary string, confirm a permitted user can retrieve it and a non-permitted user cannot, delete it, then confirm no search, cache or summary returns the canary.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Audit retrievals.\u003c/b> Log which chunk ids were retrieved for which user, so you can answer \"who could have seen this?\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prefer source-system search\u003c/b> for highly sensitive data: calling the source's own search API as a tool inherits its permissions for free, at some cost in quality.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must permission filtering happen inside the retrieval query rather than after it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Filtering after retrieval can return nothing when the best matches are forbidden, and forbidden chunks may already have passed through rerankers, prompts, logs or caches; filtering inside the search guarantees the system only ever handles chunks the user may see.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do content-addressed chunk ids make incremental indexing cheap?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A chunk's id comes from its document id and a hash of its text, so after an edit, unchanged chunks keep their ids and are skipped, only chunks with new text are embedded and inserted, and ids that no longer appear are deleted.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A user's access to a confidential project is revoked. List three places their access could persist in a RAG system.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Chunk ACL metadata until the next permission sync; semantic or response caches keyed without the permission set; derived artefacts such as summaries, community reports or graph facts built from the project's documents; and conversation memory from earlier sessions.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:advrag/choosing-a-retrieval-architecture":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>There is no best retrieval architecture, only the cheapest one that answers your users' questions. Start with a strong baseline (good parsing and chunking, hybrid search, reranking, citations), classify the questions it fails, and add the pattern that fixes the largest failure class. Every step up the ladder costs indexing money, latency or complexity, so each must earn its place in an A/B eval.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The ladder\u003c/h4>\u003cp>From cheapest to most expensive, roughly:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>No retrieval: long context.\u003c/b> If the whole knowledge base is under about 200,000 tokens (around 500 pages), Anthropic's advice is to try putting it all in the prompt with prompt caching. Compare against RAG on your eval: long prompts cost more per call and recall degrades as inputs grow (context rot, in the Context module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Hybrid search plus reranking.\u003c/b> BM25 plus vectors, fused, then a cross-encoder (the Embeddings and RAG modules). The default for most products.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Contextual retrieval.\u003c/b> Prepend a short, LLM-written context to each chunk before indexing. Anthropic reported failed retrievals (1 minus recall@20) falling by 49% with contextual embeddings plus contextual BM25, and by 67% with reranking added.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Late interaction or page-image retrieval\u003c/b> for precision on complex queries or visually rich documents.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Agentic RAG\u003c/b> for multi-hop, vague and comparison questions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Knowledge graphs, GraphRAG, LightRAG\u003c/b> for relationship, aggregation and whole-corpus questions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deep research\u003c/b> for open-ended, high-value research questions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Diagnose first\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Take 50 to 100 failed or poor answers from your eval or production traces and label each with one cause:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Failure class\u003c/th>\u003cth>How you recognise it\u003c/th>\u003cth>Usual fix\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieval miss\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The answer exists in the corpus but no retrieved chunk contains it\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Better chunking, hybrid search, contextual retrieval, reranking\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Query mismatch\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Users' words differ from the documents' words\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Query rewriting, agentic search with reformulation\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Multi-hop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Needs facts from two or more places, chained\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agentic RAG, a knowledge graph\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Aggregation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"How many\", \"which all\", \"list every\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Structured extraction into a table plus SQL, a graph\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Global\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Main themes\", \"overall trends\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>GraphRAG global search, map-reduce summarisation\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Visual\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Answer is in a chart, table image or diagram\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Captioning, ColPali-style page retrieval, VLM generation\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Stale or forbidden\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Old version or wrong permissions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Incremental sync, ACL filters\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Generation error\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Right chunks retrieved, wrong answer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Prompting, model choice, context ordering (not a retrieval problem)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The distribution tells you what to build. If 60% of failures are retrieval misses, a knowledge graph is the wrong project.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The decision table\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Situation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Architecture\u003c/th>\u003cth>Index cost\u003c/th>\u003cth>Query latency\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Small, stable corpus (under ~200k tokens)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Long context with caching\u003c/td>\u003ctd>None\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low to medium\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Help centre, policies, docs; lookup questions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hybrid plus rerank, contextual retrieval\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>PDFs dominated by charts, tables, slides\u003c/td>\u003ctd>ColPali-style or captioning, VLM answers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Multi-step questions across documents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agentic RAG over hybrid search\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium to high\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Dependencies, ownership, org structure\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Knowledge graph (often an existing one) plus tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low to medium\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"What are the themes across everything?\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>GraphRAG global search or map-reduce\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Facts that change over time, agent memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Temporal graph (Graphiti-style)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Counts and filters over many documents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Extract fields into a table, query with SQL\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Open-ended research reports\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Deep research agent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Low (uses search)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Minutes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A legal team asks for \"an assistant over 12,000 supplier contracts\". Their first 40 real questions break down as: 15 lookups (\"What is the liability cap in the Acme contract?\"), 18 aggregations (\"Which contracts renew automatically with less than 30 days' notice?\"), 5 multi-hop (\"Which suppliers of plant 7 have change-of-control clauses?\"), 2 global (\"What are the common non-standard clauses?\").\u003c/p>\u003cp>A pure RAG system will fail almost every aggregation question: no top-k set contains all 12,000 contracts. The right design is mostly \u003cb>not\u003c/b> retrieval: extract key fields (party, renewal terms, notice period, liability cap, change of control) from every contract once into a table with an LLM and structured outputs, validate a sample by hand, and let the assistant query it with SQL. Hybrid RAG with reranking handles lookups, with an agentic loop for the multi-hop ones that combine SQL results with clause text. The two global questions do not justify GraphRAG; a one-off map-reduce summary answers them.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Route by question type.\u003c/b> Mature systems classify each question (lookup, aggregate, multi-hop, global) and send it to a different pipeline instead of forcing one architecture on everything.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Measure the full trade-off.\u003c/b> For each candidate, record recall@k, answer correctness, p50 and p95 latency, cost per question and indexing cost and time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Revisit yearly.\u003c/b> Long-context prices and quality, embedding models and rerankers keep improving; a pattern that was necessary last year may not be now.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">70% of your RAG failures are \"the answer is in the corpus but not in the top 10\". What do you try first, and what do you not build?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Improve the retrieval baseline: chunking, hybrid search, contextual retrieval and a reranker, measured with recall@k. Do not start a knowledge graph or GraphRAG project, because those fix multi-hop, aggregation and global failures, not plain retrieval misses.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is \"which contracts renew automatically with under 30 days' notice?\" a poor fit for RAG, and what fits?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It needs every matching contract, not the top k similar chunks, so retrieval will miss most of them. Extract the renewal fields from all contracts once into a structured table, validate it, and answer with a SQL query (exposed to the assistant as a tool).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When is skipping retrieval entirely a reasonable choice?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When the whole knowledge base fits comfortably in the context window (roughly under 200,000 tokens) and an eval shows long-context answers are as good as RAG at acceptable cost with prompt caching; it removes chunking and retrieval failure modes completely.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:advrag/graphrag-versus-plain-rag-on-global-questions":"\u003cp>You will build a GraphRAG index over a short novel, then ask the same questions with GraphRAG's global and local search and with its \u003ccode>basic\u003c/code> mode, which is plain top-k vector RAG over the same chunks. The goal is to see with your own eyes where each wins and what the graph costs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.10 to 3.12 (the versions GraphRAG supports), about 1 GB of disk\u003c/li>\u003cli>An OpenAI or Azure OpenAI API key (the providers GraphRAG's quickstart covers), with a spending limit of a few dollars. Indexing a 40,000-token book with a small model costs well under that, but set the limit anyway\u003c/li>\u003cli>The corpus: Charles Dickens' \u003ci>A Christmas Carol\u003c/i> from Project Gutenberg (about 32,000 words), or 20 to 50 of your own documents in plain text\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Create the project and install GraphRAG:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>mkdir graphrag_lab &amp;&amp; cd graphrag_lab\npython -m venv .venv &amp;&amp; source .venv/bin/activate\npython -m pip install graphrag pandas\ngraphrag init\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>When \u003ccode>init\u003c/code> asks for models, choose a small, cheap chat model and an embedding model. It creates \u003ccode>.env\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>settings.yaml\u003c/code> and an \u003ccode>input\u003c/code> folder.\u003c/p>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Put your key in \u003ccode>.env\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>GRAPHRAG_API_KEY=...\u003c/code>). Open \u003ccode>settings.yaml\u003c/code>, find the \u003ccode>models:\u003c/code> section and confirm the chat and embedding models are the ones you want to pay for.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Add the corpus:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>curl https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/24022/pg24022.txt -o ./input/book.txt\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"4\">\u003cli>Index it and time the run:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>time graphrag index\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Watch the pipeline steps scroll past: chunking, entity and relationship extraction, description summarisation, community detection, community reports, embeddings. Note the wall-clock time and, afterwards, the token usage on your provider's dashboard.\u003c/p>\u003col start=\"5\">\u003cli>Look inside the index:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import glob\nimport pandas as pd\n\nfor path in sorted(glob.glob(\"output/*.parquet\")):\n    df = pd.read_parquet(path)\n    print(f\"{path}: {len(df)} rows; columns: {list(df.columns)[:8]}\")\n\nreports = pd.read_parquet(glob.glob(\"output/*community_reports*.parquet\")[0])\nprint(reports.iloc[0].to_dict())   # one community report: title, summary, findings\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Read two or three community reports. They are the precomputed summaries that global search will use.\u003c/p>\u003col start=\"6\">\u003cli>Write ten questions in \u003ccode>questions.txt\u003c/code>, one per line: five \u003cb>global\u003c/b> and five \u003cb>local\u003c/b> (lookup). For \u003ci>A Christmas Carol\u003c/i>, for example:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>What are the main themes of this story?\nHow does the story portray wealth and poverty?\nWhat role do the supernatural visitors play in the story as a whole?\nHow does the mood of the story change from beginning to end?\nWhat are the most important relationships in the story?\nWho was Jacob Marley?\nWho is Bob Cratchit's employer?\nWho is Fezziwig?\nWhat does Scrooge send to the Cratchit family on Christmas morning?\nWhat does the last spirit show Scrooge about Tiny Tim?\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"7\">\u003cli>Ask every question three ways and save the answers:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>for m in basic local global; do\n  : &gt; answers_$m.md\n  while IFS= read -r q; do\n    echo \"## $q\" &gt;&gt; answers_$m.md\n    graphrag query \"$q\" --method $m &gt;&gt; answers_$m.md\n  done &lt; questions.txt\ndone\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Time one global and one basic query with \u003ccode>time\u003c/code> to compare latency.\u003c/p>\u003col start=\"8\">\u003cli>Judge the answers. For each question, read the \u003ccode>basic\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>global\u003c/code> answers side by side without looking at which is which (ask a friend to shuffle them, or paste them in random order). Score each pair on the GraphRAG paper's criteria: \u003cb>comprehensiveness\u003c/b> (covers all aspects), \u003cb>diversity\u003c/b> (varied perspectives) and \u003cb>directness\u003c/b> (answers the question specifically and concisely). Then check the five lookup answers for correctness.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003col start=\"9\">\u003cli>Fill in a results table:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>                    basic        local        global\nGlobal Qs won       _/5          _/5          _/5\nLookups correct     _/5          _/5          _/5\nSeconds per query   _            _            _\nIndex cost: ___ tokens, ___ minutes (basic alone needs only the embeddings)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Global questions:\u003c/b> global search gives broader, better-organised answers that draw on the whole book; basic search answers from whichever few chunks happened to match, often missing major themes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Lookups:\u003c/b> basic and local search answer correctly and directly; global search is slower, wordier and occasionally vaguer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost:\u003c/b> indexing makes dozens to a few hundred LLM calls even for one short book, versus a few embedding calls for basic search, and each global query makes several calls (map over report batches, then reduce). Multiply by your real corpus size before proposing GraphRAG at work.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which of your questions would justify the cost of GraphRAG in a real product, and why?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Only the whole-corpus questions such as main themes, overall trends or important relationships, where precomputed community summaries let the model cover the entire corpus; lookups were answered as well or better by basic search at a fraction of the index and query cost, so a real product should route them to plain RAG.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>--community-level\u003c/code> change in global search?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Which level of the community hierarchy the reports come from: lower-level, more detailed communities give more thorough answers but mean more reports to map over, so more LLM calls, time and cost; higher-level communities are cheaper and broader.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:advrag/atlas-m8b-retrieval-upgrade-measured":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Starting point: Atlas after M8.\u003c/b> Atlas has a provider-agnostic client with cost logging (M1, M2), versioned prompts (M3), typed tools (M4), a context assembler (M5), memory (M6), hybrid BM25 plus vector search with reciprocal rank fusion (M7) and grounded answers with citations plus a RAG eval (M8).\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>This milestone\u003c/b> adds three retrieval upgrades, each behind a config flag: \u003cb>contextual retrieval\u003c/b> (an LLM-written context prepended to every chunk before indexing), a \u003cb>cross-encoder reranker\u003c/b>, and an \u003cb>agentic search mode\u003c/b> in which the model calls search as a tool. You measure every combination against M8 on the same labelled questions and ship only what clears your bar. The deliverable is as much the evidence as the code: a results table and a one-page decision memo.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>An eval set of 60 to 100 questions, extended from M8's, each with relevant chunk or document ids, a reference answer and a type tag: \u003ccode>lookup\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>multi-hop\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>vague\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>unanswerable\u003c/code> (at least 10 of each of the last three).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Four variants selectable by config: \u003ccode>baseline\u003c/code> (M8), \u003ccode>ctx\u003c/code> (contextual chunks), \u003ccode>ctx+rerank\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ctx+rerank+agent\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Contextual retrieval: for every chunk, a small model writes 50 to 100 tokens situating it in its document; prepend it before both embedding and BM25 indexing. Use prompt caching with the document as the cached prefix, and log indexing tokens and cost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reranker: retrieve 50 to 100 candidates, rerank with a cross-encoder (hosted or a local open model), pass the top 5 to 10 to the generator.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Agentic mode: \u003ccode>search_docs\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>read_document\u003c/code> tools over your hybrid search, at most 5 searches, the no-new-evidence stop rule and a forced final answer that states what is missing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Metrics per variant and per question type: recall@10 against labelled ids, answer correctness with an LLM judge validated against at least 30 of your own labels (report agreement), citation accuracy, correct abstention on unanswerable questions, p50 and p95 latency, cost per question, and one-off indexing cost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>One command runs the full matrix and writes the report.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Freeze the baseline.\u003c/b> Tag the M8 commit. Run the M8 eval three times and record the spread of each metric: that is your noise floor.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Extend the eval set.\u003c/b> Add multi-hop, vague and unanswerable questions from real usage or realistic guesses; label relevant ids by searching the corpus yourself.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Contextual index.\u003c/b> Build a second index alongside the first (never overwrite the baseline). Generate contexts with prompt caching, record tokens, cost and time, and spot-check 20 generated contexts by hand.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reranker.\u003c/b> Add the rerank stage with configurable candidate and output counts; measure its latency separately.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Agentic mode.\u003c/b> Implement the tool loop with budgets and logging of every query the model writes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Run the matrix.\u003c/b> Every variant, every question, at least twice. Produce the table, broken down by question type.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Decide.\u003c/b> Read 10 traces where variants disagreed. Write the memo: chosen default, cost per 1,000 questions, latency impact, and when (if ever) to use agentic mode.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>One command (for example \u003ccode>python -m atlas.eval --matrix\u003c/code>) rebuilds the results from scratch\u003c/li>\u003cli>The report shows each variant's quality, latency and cost side by side, per question type, with the baseline noise floor marked\u003c/li>\u003cli>At least five concrete examples where a variant fixed or broke an answer, with retrieved ids and traces\u003c/li>\u003cli>Atlas's default configuration is the variant your memo recommends, and the memo states the evidence\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A router that sends lookups to one-shot RAG and multi-hop or vague questions to agentic mode; report the blended cost and quality\u003c/li>\u003cli>ColPali-style page retrieval for the PDF-heavy part of your corpus, evaluated on questions about charts and tables\u003c/li>\u003cli>LightRAG or GraphRAG over a subset, for a handful of whole-corpus questions\u003c/li>\u003cli>Permission filters on chunks plus the canary deletion test from the production concerns lesson\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Expect the biggest gains on \u003ccode>vague\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>multi-hop\u003c/code> questions from agentic mode, and on \u003ccode>lookup\u003c/code> recall from contextual chunks plus reranking. If a gain is smaller than your noise floor, it is not a gain.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Anthropic's own worked estimate for contextual retrieval, with prompt caching and their 2024 prices, was $1.02 per million document tokens; recompute it with your model's current prices and chunk sizes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep the generator model and prompt identical across variants, so you measure retrieval, not generation.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
